 en:{story:'I slept pretty well last night! But I needed a little extra time to recover after play today.',storyGood:'I had a steady day! My breathing, heart and rest were close to my usual routine.',storyEvidence:'Recovery ~45 min, compared with my typical ~29 min. Simulated reading.',attention:'Recovery after activity took 16 minutes longer than usual. Watch if the pattern continues.',ageIntro:'This is a model demonstration; longer-term trends matter more than a single value.',nightQuote:'I got some quiet rest last night. My resting indicators stayed near usual.',nightSub:'Nighttime heart and breathing trends · valid data only',rec1:'Recovery after exercise',rec1why:'For a large dog, recovery trends matter. Today took longer than this dog’s baseline.',rec1what:'Compare recovery after similar activity, rather than interpreting one day.',rec2:'Maintain healthy body condition',rec2why:'Breed, age 4, and ~28 kg suggest regular weight and body condition tracking.',rec2what:'Log weight and body condition weekly; review the food target.',rec3:'Maintain steady rest',rec3why:'Nightly resting trends help form a personal reference.',rec3what:'Monitor a consistent routine for 14–30 days.',rec4:'Consistent nutrition',rec4why:'Food targets should be assessed alongside actual intake and body condition.',rec4what:'Log meals and verify the food’s energy density.',rec5:'Keep care context accurate',rec5why:'Reports and medication timelines contextualize physiological changes.',rec5what:'Verify uncertain report items and keep medication records updated.',insight:'This demo trend compares the pet with its own baseline and is not a diagnosis.',ageDetail:'Biological age is a conceptual estimate using age, body size, long-term activity, rest and cardiopulmonary patterns. The displayed 4.2 is demo data, not a clinically validated age test.'},
